  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "phoebe-letter",
  "text": "Paul opens the most famous letter in the New Testament by telling an entire church to give this woman whatever she asks for.\n\nRomans sixteen. The final chapter. And Paul starts it with a personal commendation.\n\n\"I commend unto you Phebe our sister, which is a servant of the church which is at Cenchrea.\"\n\nNow, that word servant. In Greek it's diakonos. Elsewhere in the New Testament translators render that same word deacon, or minister. Here, historically, a lot of them chose servant. Scholars have gone back and forth on it for centuries and you should know that's a translation decision, not a settled fact.\n\nThen Paul asks the Roman church to receive her, and, \"that ye assist her in whatsoever business she hath need of you.\"\n\nWhatever she needs. Give it to her.\n\nAnd then his reason. \"For she hath been a succourer of many, and of myself also.\"\n\nA patron. A protector. She had backed Paul personally.\n\nOne more thing, and I want to be straight with you. You'll often hear that Phoebe carried the letter to Rome. That is a very old and very reasonable inference, because she's commended first, right at the front. It is not stated in the text. Inference, not chapter and verse.",
